1d:["$","p",null,{"children":["The ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"client-facing API"}]," is the Remote Execution API. It is a published,\nversioned protocol, and Bazel talking to Forge over ",["$","code",null,{"children":":50051"}]," and ",["$","code",null,{"children":":50052"}],"\nis the most stable surface here. You do not generally need to upgrade clients in\nstep with the server."]}]
1e:["$","p",null,{"children":["The ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"config schema"}]," is the surface that moves. Fields get added constantly,\nwhich is harmless; occasionally one is renamed or removed, which is not."]}]
1f:["$","p",null,{"children":["The ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"worker API"}]," between a worker and the scheduler is internal. Nothing\npromises that a worker from one release talks to a scheduler from another, so\ntreat scheduler and workers as a unit that upgrades together."]}]
20:["$","p",null,{"children":["Stored ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"data"}]," is content-addressed, which does most of the work for you: a\nblob's key is its digest, so bytes written by an old version are found by a new\none. What is not automatically portable is the on-disk or in-Redis ",["$","em",null,{"children":"layout"}]," a\nstore uses to organize those bytes. Layout changes are rare (the filesystem\nstore still migrates its old single-folder layout in place at startup), and they\nare one of the things to look for when you read the changelog."]}]
